{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449583-have-you-used-the-s\u00edoch\u00e1in","id":1449583,"title":"Have you used the S\u00edoch\u00e1in?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-25T13:51:40-07:00","id":1449583,"title":"Have you used the S\u00edoch\u00e1in?","content":"Ever used the S\u00edoch\u00e1in, either as NPCs or giving players the chance to play one? How did it go?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-25T13:54:40-07:00","id":1449584,"title":"","content":"I really want to,but I'm having a hard time finding a game","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-25T18:40:01-07:00","id":1449597,"title":"","content":"They are neat, and seem best used as NPCs. If I ever get a Changeling game going, I will make a couple as protagonists and antagonists. Someone who embodies the selfish overlord archetype, or a group that embodies the scheming cabal archetype, could be interesting. Also, a wandering merchant who sells you just what you need when your group is ready to advance is also a archetype idea. Like a book that gives you the basics on the Art you want to start studying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-26T04:29:01-07:00","id":1449640,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Yes I did, and not any S\u00edochain, the first one. This was in second edition, before the c20 players guide, so there was no rules for S\u00edochains, at the time my take on it was that S\u00edochains merged their fae and mortal sides into a single being, and this would require both a deep understanding of yourself and completely balancing glamour and banality. My take wasn\u2019t that far off after all, but initially I didn\u2019t like the rules for the C20 players guide, because I was fond of my own, but they weren\u2019t bad and eventually they\u2019ve grown on me,I like the mantle idea. But anyway the first s\u00edochain wasa ghille dhu of all kiths, the kith most vulnerable to banality. She believes banality is not a bad thing, glamour is marvellous, but without banality glamour is pure chaos, infinite possibilities and no form. She believes that now we are living a time of imbalance where there is too much banality, but that in the past we lived another kind of imbalance, when there was too much glamour. During our chronicle the motley sought her help, but had to go from concordia to hibernia for it (ireland) and finally to Hy Brazil, the mythical island where she resides. She taught them a new art (no name for it in english yet) which allows them to purify things, they used it to purify a balefire back home. </span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I'm actually writing a storyteller\u2019s vault material that talks a bit about S\u00edochain, the book is actually focused on totems, and how europeans changelings view and use them, since this was only touched upon regarding nunnehi and menehune, the kithain actually refers to totems as \u201cemblems of power\u201d in the archaic way. In this book I also present the chimerical totems, which are thematically very different from the umbral totems (both are followed by changelings thought), in this book the s\u00edochains were responsible for popularizing totems among the european kithain during the shattering - resurgence period. In fact one thing that I touch upon in this book is how totem worship is one of the possible paths to become a s\u00edochain. Basically some worshipers can understand the concept of their totems so well that they might assume the mantle of it, or a mantle related to it. I\u2019ve made a small list of famous s\u00edochains who took this path and how it was. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I\u2019d appreciate any feedback, I'm kinda finishing the book right now, trying to improve on what I wrote to finally release it. </span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ysabel - The White Queen</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mantle: Evil Queen</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Born from autumn world nobility, she lived a pampered infancy just to discover she was a commoner after passing through the chrysalis. She was extremely excited to discover her true nature as a sluagh, but at the same point disappointed that her mortal blood didn\u2019t mean a lot in changeling society. She joined the remnants of house Eiluned during the interregnum, seeking some form of nobility. She became an accomplished spymaster and earned the title of baroness, but she wanted more, she wanted to be a queen. Seeking more power Ysabel entered the crystal circle and learned about the Solar Crown totem, she followed it relentlessly, hoping to rise in the ranks of house Eiluned, but she soon realized that it was all futile, real power wasn\u2019t granted, it was conquered. She cast aside her allegiance to house Eiluned along with her meaningless title and set off to become a real queen. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">After a long journey Ysabel became a S\u00edoch\u00e1in without any help, and took the mantle of evil queen. Using her trickery and sheer power she created the kingdom of the snowy mountains in the pyrenees, the first kingdom created by a non-sidhe during the interregnum. She ruffled a few feathers, from both commoners who wanted no queen and autumn sidhe outraged at her arrogance, but she proved a worthy opponent and even after the resurgence her small kingdom endured. The mantle demands that she moves around constantly, travelling to other kingdoms and wrecking political havok just for her pleasure, leaving her kingdom to be overseen by her villainous regent. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appearance: Ysabel uses only the most luxurious voiles, everything she uses it white, even her hair is white, contrasting with her eyes which are pitch black. It\u2019s easy to forget she can only whisper because whenever she speaks everyone goes silent, making her words crystal clear even in a ballroom. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Flower - The Simpleton</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mantle: Wise Fool</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Flower is one of the disciples of Elowen, a young boggan that always has a blank stare and the shadow of a smile on her lips. Right after the chrysalis she was adopted by a big motley of boggans, and lived a happy life, their freehold was big, comfy and safe. Her biggest pleasure was to make people laugh, no matter how, even if she had to ridicule herself, Flower always thought that as long as those around her could laugh, then everything was alright with the world. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">She decided to follow the Rainbowfall totem simply because the name was pretty, which was very fitting. Flower always took spontaneity to a new level, even the most ardent followers of the Rainbowfall sometimes second guessed themselves, but never Flower, her cheerful demeanor and simple behaviour always won the hearts of those around her. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Flower drew Elowen attention, who saw in the boggan potential and in her name some significance. The S\u00edoch\u00e1in helped the wilder into her journey, but never directly, she needed only the smallest pushes to go in the right direction. As the Wise Fool, Flower still acts almost the same, but her pranks cause not only laughter, but pondering, very often she outwits scheming grumps by apparent luck, and teaches all sorts of valuable lessons to those who can see past the image of a fool. But even those who see in her some hidden wisdom never realise she was a s\u00edoch\u00e1in. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appearance: Flower has a round face that makes her look chubby even though she isn\u2019t, her hair is short and shiny like spun-gold and her big eyebrows are curly. Her eyes are hazel and transmit a vague happiness all the time. She always dresses with mismatched colorful voiles, but never as an actual fool. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Jankin - The knight of spoons</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mantle: Cupid</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This satyr tried a bit of everything when it comes to celebrations. He adopted the fe\u00e9 verte totem early and the parties he promoted impressed the local nobles, his celebrations were more than simple debauchery, he had a mind for unique and interesting events, and a special talent to bring out the inner desires of everyone present, but also the ability to make everything go smoothly, preserving reputations. The nobles knighted him, because it was easier to excuse themselves to the party of an honorable knight instead of a small time satyr. This made him jokingly remark how he became a knight without ever touching a sword, but that his ability with absinthe spoons was legendary. The joke caught on and he became known as the knight of spoons. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Eventually everyone wanted him to organize their own parties, and his reputation grew. As he became older he started to find more joy in planning instead of partying. He understood expectations like no one else, and knew how to keep the flow of the party. The masked balls he organized were truly special, there all guests felt like equals, commoners and nobles could see eye to eye, and talk to each other unmasked of all pretense. He saw many forbidden relationships bloom during his masquerades, just to be pruned down outside of the party. He took to himself the objective of making these romances possible, planning his parties methodically to make sure love would win in the end. This attracted Elowen's attention, and she helped Jankin to unveil his full potential, becoming a s\u00edoch\u00e1in. He threw one last legendary party that is still talked about today. There he abdicated his ceremonial spoon, symbol of his knightly status, and disappeared mysteriously at the height of the party. Since then Jankin has dedicated himself fully to the cupid mantle, making sure no love is forbidden. Parties are still one of his favoured methods to help lovers, but he also uses many other tricks. Like Elowen he rarely shows his true identity. </span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26021,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","user":"Rucun","date":". 05-26-2021, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-26T11:43:00-07:00","id":1449677,"title":"","content":"No - I think Golconda thematically works as a &quot;salvation&quot; state because those who seek it perceive the vampiric experience as a negative. Changelings fundamentally see their state of being as a positive and are desperately clinging to it, against forces that would strip them of it. I don't think escape from that tension serves the setting. But that's personal taste.<br /> <br /> That said, as a (false) rumor in setting, that sends characters after a centuries old changeling, who turns out to be a Bedlam-lost lunatic who actually survived by hiding in glamour, works fine <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":1098,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","user":"glamourweaver","date":". 05-26-2021, 02:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-27T12:25:17-07:00","id":1449855,"title":"","content":"I've been reading the Fables comics and it struck me that the S\u00edoch\u00e1in could fill a niche in the setting, as characters from fairy tales seem to be missing from the Dreaming and Changeling society as a concept. If the S\u00edoch\u00e1in were more common instead of something rare - which granted, the rules in the Players Guide do allow - more Fable-like characters could appear in a game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-05T15:11:33-07:00","id":1451077,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449583-have-you-used-the-s\u00edoch\u00e1in?p=1449855#post1449855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been reading the Fables comics and it struck me that the S\u00edoch\u00e1in could fill a niche in the setting, as characters from fairy tales seem to be missing from the Dreaming and Changeling society as a concept. If the S\u00edoch\u00e1in were more common instead of something rare - which granted, the rules in the Players Guide do allow - more Fable-like characters could appear in a game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019m all for characters fitting fairy tale roles, but I\u2019m not seeing the Siochain connection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-08T11:41:19-07:00","id":1451362,"title":"","content":"I was thinking that a Mantle could be for a specific fairy tale character. Some would have only one Fae associated with the Mantle, while other Mantles could have historically belonged to several successive fae, or to multiple fae at the same time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-08T14:38:03-07:00","id":1451388,"title":"","content":"Many Fairy Tale characters fit archetypes. <br /> <br /> &quot;The Boy Who Set Out to Learn What Fear is&quot; is a perfect Fool of Fortune.<br /> <br /> Snow White is a Child of the Three Colors.<br /> <br /> Cinderella (fire elf) is a tale of initiation and transformation. <br /> <br /> The Miller's daughter in &quot;Rumplestillskin&quot; (the real with quiet skin) has clearly been set impossible tasks, much like Psyche. The poor woman has to not only spin straw into gold, she must spin lies into truth.<br /> <br /> Think of the Brave Little Tailor who defeats brutal monsters with his wits. <br /> <br /> Giant Killer is an archetype. So the archetype in &quot;Jack the Giant Killer&quot; is obvious.<br /> <br /> It makes perfect sense that Siochain would take on the roles of storybook heroes. By now a bold brave Bogan childling has taken on the mantle of Dorothy. Let tyrants quake in their boots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T19:28:18-07:00","id":1451518,"title":"","content":"Thanks, <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\">Astromancer</a>!</b> You managed to put into words what I had in mind, but couldn't express it myself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T22:50:31-07:00","id":1451524,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449583-have-you-used-the-s\u00edoch\u00e1in?p=1451077#post1451077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019m all for characters fitting fairy tale roles, but I\u2019m not seeing the Siochain connection.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the idea is that such Siochain don't fit fairy tale <i>roles</i>, but that they are the specific fairy character in that fairy tale or legend. That way characters can meet and interact with the actual Rumplestiltskin, the Puss in Boots, the fairy godmothers of Cinderella, the Queen of the Fairies from Tam Lin, etc. who would be effectively permanent fixtures of the setting or chronicle.<br /> <br /> There's a stock character called the Devil's Grandmother in various fairy tales, and I've been intending on including this extremely ancient fae in one of my chronicles (not Changeling, but intersecting with it). I didn't really give it much thought as to the exact nature of the character in Changeling's increasingly complex system of Kithain, Thallain, Adhene, Chimera, etc. But the immortal Sicohain might provide a good fit. I wouldn't have thought of it without valismedsen's post (if that was not his intent, hey I got some creative use out of it anyway!).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T01:27:58-07:00","id":1451619,"title":"","content":"Black Fox, that's exactly what I had in mind!<br /> <br /> Also, now I'm thinking how some fairy tales characters are quite easy to envision as S\u00edoch\u00e1in changeling, for instance, a Pooka Puss in Boots. Others, those that are humans in their original stories, I'm not sure how to portray. Most moral fable protagonists are described as mundane, actually encountering supernatural beings rather than being supernatural. Baba Yaga might be a vicious Redcap S\u00edoch\u00e1in, but what kith could Vasilisa be? Granted, not all of the characters need to be S\u00edoch\u00e1in, but it would be interesting to meet Snow White, for instance. I guess they could have been originaly Kinain... Would it be possible for Kinain to become S\u00edoch\u00e1in, maybe?<br /> <br /> Also, how much Glamour a S\u00edoch\u00e1in could accrue from the perennial inspiration to Reverie their stories are? There should be a Background for that!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T02:44:31-07:00","id":1451624,"title":"","content":"Tried once, but was not totaly successful.<br /> <br /> I tried a Mantle: Immortal Bard. A beeing who knows ancient tales and legends and sees the current ones unfold, but unable/willing to affect them.<br /> <br /> I gave some information to the Characters related to an incoming cataclysmic event, but it made them even more confused than they were before.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/562-lachdanan","name":"Lachdanan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar562_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T18:38:18-07:00","id":1452101,"title":"","content":"Was being a Siochain that did not help the Npc communicate clearly with the players? How did you present the character? For instance, where did they live, in the chronicle's city? In the Dreaming?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-13T19:42:19-07:00","id":1452103,"title":"","content":"There is an archetype out there s/he has had many names Oedipus, Jason, Theseus, Cinderella, in the twentieth Century we named them Dorothy Gale and John Carter of Mars. They achieve impressive mental and physical feats but they walk funny. Theseus wore gold sandals, Cinderella glass slippers, and Dorothy silver. Oedipus had wounded feet. Jason wore one sandal when he was recognized as king and Dorothy one slipper when she killed the Witch of the West. <br /> Oedipus slew crowds of men that attacked him. Theseus moved stones no other mortal could shift. Jason won many tests of speed and strength. Cinderella climbed trees no one else could, ran through the Swan cot unharmed and was the only one to get down the pitch covered stairs. Dorothy's physical endurance over weeks of travel by foot is astounding for a young child. John Carter of Mars was superstrong on Mars. <br /> Oedipus, Theseus, Jason, all solved insoluble riddles. Cinderella spoke the language of birds. Dorothy and John Carter brought hidden truths to light with revolutionary force. <br /> Carlo Ginzburg says they're all part of one ancient story we do and don't know. I think he's on to something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T07:01:08-07:00","id":1452161,"title":"","content":"Really interesting, <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer\">Astromancer</a>! </b>Would you reccomend a starting point to get into Ginzburg's work?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/412-valismedsen","name":"valismedsen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar412_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T11:30:10-07:00","id":1452187,"title":"","content":"The Worms and the Cheese is generally seen as a starting point. Let me look up a few more titles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T11:45:03-07:00","id":1452194,"title":"","content":"Stray thought. Harlequin literally means &quot;Hell's Knight.&quot; He's an ancient trickster and force of chaos. The name points to a figure expressing the will of the other world. His lover Columbine (the dove) could be seen as a type of the soul. Pierrot, spinner, also vaugely suggests ritual movement. <br /> <br /> I once read a Victorian scholar who proposed that the figures of the Comedia del Arte represented an ancient religious rite that lived on as a performance. He was probably wrong, but what a theme for a group of Siochain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 06-17-2021, 08:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-14T22:19:11-07:00","id":1452277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>valismedsen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449583-have-you-used-the-s\u00edoch\u00e1in?p=1451619#post1451619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, now I'm thinking how some fairy tales characters are quite easy to envision as S\u00edoch\u00e1in changeling, for instance, a Pooka Puss in Boots. Others, those that are humans in their original stories, I'm not sure how to portray. Most moral fable protagonists are described as mundane, actually encountering supernatural beings rather than being supernatural. Baba Yaga might be a vicious Redcap S\u00edoch\u00e1in, but what kith could Vasilisa be? Granted, not all of the characters need to be S\u00edoch\u00e1in, but it would be interesting to meet Snow White, for instance. I guess they could have been originaly Kinain... Would it be possible for Kinain to become S\u00edoch\u00e1in, maybe?</div> </div> </div> </div>I always considered such characters to be ordinary humans. In Changeling terms they were either humans who trespassed faerie lands in an age when such things were possible, or humans who were Enchanted in someway (perhaps simply by the fact that somehow trespassed faerie land, aka trods/freeholds/glens!) so that they could interact with creatures of the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> As such, I don't think they'd be naturally immortal. They would return to ordinary life grow old and die like all other humans.<br /> <br /> However, I would think that it should also be possible for certain enchanted humans to somehow be kept from aging by use of faerie sorcery of some kind. Maybe there's some faerie realm of &quot;And They Lived Happily Ever After&quot; land in the Deep Dreaming that favored mortals of the fae could be sent too, although it is likely as much a prison as a paradise. In such cases, they might be like the fae who are Lost Ones.<br /> <br /> Another option is that such people are dead, but the memories of them and their exploits are somehow retained by the Dreaming as a form of a unique Chimera. Their progenitor exploits were so great in their contribution to the Dreaming, that they spawned as their own. Such Chimera are effectively immortal as long as the stories about them continue to be told. Although this is probably not what you were looking for, this is probably how I would manage such a concept in my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> You could also bring in descendants of these original humans as Kinain. Again, not what you want. But they might make interesting additions to a cast of NPCs because that person there is the long lost descendant of Snow White, Rose Red, etc. and somehow contain their &quot;legacy&quot; (yet such faerie magic doesn't really pass from one generation to the next, but only manifests in special circumstances known only to the Dreaming to a special descendant - perhaps they look identical to the original human). Yet the faerie are in such awe of this mortal (as the heir to such an important person) that they treat them as if they were that person (in the same way that whoever becomes the next King or Queen of England or whatever, is considered to be the King or Queen despite not being the King of Queen previously). In this case, such names are now more like noble titles, and the Dreaming bestows any number of gifts/powers/lore to that person. So although there was only one original Snow White of the stories, there have been many &quot;Snow Whites&quot; ever since. Maybe that person has dreams or fantasies in which they &quot;live out&quot; those stories, and might even eventually believe they were the original person (similar to being in Bedlam). Or they may have a special destiny to fulfill, or some other special purpose. This might be a good option if all you are looking for is a quest for the PCs to save &quot;Snow White&quot; from some dire peril as opposed to simply name dropping.<br /> <br /> Some faerie tales have &quot;helpers&quot; with magic - marking them as one of the faerie - but nevertheless appear completely human. Since I never figured out how to map the Eshu kith to anything else in a fairy tale, this is where I usually slot them. That's why they go out on adventures - to help humans achieve their quest. (A good example is the gooseherder in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tattercoats\" target=\"_blank\">Tattercoats</a>).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-29T16:23:00-08:00","id":1469213,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449583-have-you-used-the-s\u00edoch\u00e1in?p=1449677#post1449677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No - I think Golconda thematically works as a &quot;salvation&quot; state because those who seek it perceive the vampiric experience as a negative. Changelings fundamentally see their state of being as a positive and are desperately clinging to it, against forces that would strip them of it. I don't think escape from that tension serves the setting. But that's personal taste.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not what the S\u00edoch\u00e1in are or represent.<br /> They represent immunity to the rigors of Banality and the risks of Bedlam.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13114-cuannan","name":"CuAnnan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}